[GER_WW2.FullName]
The Third Reich 1939-1945
[GER_WW2.Description]

[GER_WW2.Epilogue]
The state existence of Germany was terminated on May 23, 1945, the territory of the former Austria was separated from its territory (divided into 4 zones of occupation), Alsace and Lorraine (returned to France), the Sudetenland (returned to Czechoslovakia), the Eupen and Malmedy region (returned to Belgium), the statehood of Luxembourg was restored, The territories of Poland annexed in 1939 (Posen, Wartaland, part of Pomerania) were separated. Memel (Klaipeda) region was returned to the Lithuanian SSR. East Prussia is divided between the USSR and Poland. The rest is divided into 4 occupation zones - Soviet, American, British and French. The USSR transferred part of its occupation zone east of the Oder and Neisse rivers to Poland.

On July 17 – August 2, 1945, a conference of the leaders of the victorious powers was held in the Berlin suburb of Potsdam. The Soviet delegation was headed by Stalin, the American delegation by Truman, and the English delegation by Churchill (on July 28, he was replaced by the new Prime Minister Attlee). The central place was occupied by the question of the post-war structure of Europe. It was decided to preserve Germany as a single state, implement measures to disarm and demilitarize it, and completely eliminate the remnants of the fascist regime (the so-called denazification). To do this, the troops of the victorious countries (including France) had to enter the territory of Germany, and their stay was not limited. The issue of reparations payments from Germany in favor of the USSR as the country most affected by Hitler's aggression has been resolved.

[G001.FullName]
Battle of Mokra
[G001.Description]
According to the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact Eastern Europe was divided between the USSR and Germany into relative spheres of interest. This document stipulated that Poland should pass under German control.

On September 1, 1939 the German aviation struck its first air blows bombing communications junctions, aerodromes and administrative centers of Poland. German ground troops were pressing their advance on three fronts. Army Group North was delivering a blow from the Northern-Western regions of Germany and East Prussia. From the South-East of Germany and Northern Slovakia Army Group South was attacking,  supported by the Slovak 1st and 2nd Divisions. Sparse Polish troops equipped with outdated armored vehicles and aviation could not withstand the power of the Third Reich armies. The complete conquest of Poland was a matter of a few weeks.

In the area of the Mokra River, Polish troops violently resisted the German invaders, in particular the 3rd and the 4th Armies of Army Group North.

[G002.FullName]
Dunkirk
[G002.Description]
The French fortified Maginot Line went down in history as the most immense yet most inefficient military fortifications. The German command simply decided to outflank it from the north. After the invasion of Belgium on May 20, 1940 the German panzer groups began a breakthrough to Abbeville pressing troops of the 1st Group of the Allied forces in the area of Gravelines, Arras and Bruges. Troops of Army Group A started to advance against them from the West and the South-West while Army Group B was advancing from the East and the Southeast. 

At the same time the British command passed the decision to evacuate the Allied troops from the area of Dunkirk to Britain. 

On May 24, 1940 German panzer groups got an order to stop at the Betun - Saint-Omer- Gravelines line. Thus the German command unintentionally facilitated the evacuation of the Allied troops. The Wehrmacht wanted to avoid losses in tanks and reckoned on inflicting damage on the Allied troops by air strikes and a large-scale infantry attack but the Allies offered stubborn resistance to advancing German infantry.

[G004.FullName]
Road to Moscow
[G004.Description]
In October 1941 German troops got within sight of Moscow. Inspired by their earlier successes, the Wehrmacht command initially planned to have captured Moscow by September but the stubborn resistance by Soviet soldiers – as well as unfavorable weather conditions – slowed down their progress. On October 22, 1941 the German troops struck a blow directed at Chern from the area of Mtsensk but failed due to poor  coordination between artillery and panzer troops.

Sharp frosts came in, the mud froze up and use of machinery became nearly impossible. The 3rd Panzer Division operating to the Northwest of Mtsensk resumed the attack on October 23 but marshland and vast Russian minefields complicated the advance of German troops in this area.

[G005.FullName]
Battle of Dubosekovo
[G005.Description]
In November 1941, after a failure southwards of Moscow, the Wehrmacht decided to probe the Soviet troops located along the northwest defensive lines. It was at this section of the front where, by the middle of November, German tanks came as close as they ever would to the capital of the USSR.   

On November 16, 1941 units of the German Army began an advance towards the railway station of Dubosekovo, a location of strategic importance both for sides. It was a prime location for launching further attacks for the German troops as well as providing a logistic benefit by supplying ammunition and provisions to the frontline. Only units of  Soviet 316th Rifle Division, already battered from previous combat, defended the station. To make matters worse, the Soviet troops lacked tanks and anti-tank resources. It seemed that nothing could stop the Germans.

[G006.FullName]
Battle of Seliger
[G006.Description]
As a result of the situational development on the front in early January 1942 the Soviet 3rd and 4th Shock Attack Armies took up defensive dispositions along the line of Lake Seliger, Ostashkov, and the   Northern bank of Lake Volgo. German troops did not expect activity from any Soviet units and had just three Infantry Divisions and a Cavalry Brigade of Army Group North committed and those units were spread out over some 100 km. According to the German plan of attack, they would penetrate into the area of Lake Seliger located deep inside the Russian area of control. Soviet troops however, had built up a very powerful defense in the area of the village of Krivaya Kletka located on the banks of Lake Seliger. In this area, the Russians had also established a vast bridgehead to prepare for a counter-attack aimed to strike at the gap between German Army Groups North and Center.

[G008.FullName]
Operation Citadel 
[G008.Description]
For several months the opposing forces held off with any major actions. Both sides used this time to regroup their forces and pull in their reserves. During this short respite, the German General Staff was drafting the plan for Operation Citadel.

What follows is a portion of the general order issued for the commencement of Operation Citadel:

Fuhrer Staff, April 15, 1943 
Top Secret
Command Staff Only
To be transmitted via the designated officer only

Operational Order №6 

As soon as the weather conditions are favorable, Operation Citadel shall be launched. 

This advance is especially important. It shall be carried out quick and with a lot of power, transferring the initiative to us for the spring and summer of the current year. 

With that in mind, all preparations were made with a lot of care and energy. Our best detachments are to be used in all the key areas, the best weapons, best commanders, a good deal of ammo. Every commander, and every soldier shall have the complete understanding of the decisive meaning of this advance. The victory at Kursk shall light the torch for the entire world. 

With regard to the above said, here are the orders: 

1. The purpose of the advance is to haul a massive, merciless, and swift strike by each of the advancing armies from Belgorod and Orel areas, encircle the enemy forces concentrated under Kursk and completely eliminate them. The advance should result in our forces taking over a shorter and power-saving frontline. 

2. It is absolutely necessary to: 

a) Ensure the unexpectedness of the strike, and most of all, keep the enemy unaware of the date of the advance. 

b) Carefully concentrate the advance forces in a narrow part of the front to use the advantage in materiel (tanks, assault guns, artillery, smoke mortars, etc.) break through the enemy lines and encircle the enemy, already before the two advancing armies meet. 

c) Push the advancing storm wedges forward to cover our flanks as quickly as possible and so that the wedges could continue the attack. 

d) Continuously strike at the entrapped enemy from all sides and thus expedite the elimination. 
<...>
e) Advance with sufficient speed so as to ensure that the enemy does not escape the encirclement, nor has a chance to pull in the reserves from other fronts. 

g) By quickly establishing a new front line, free up the forces, especially mobile troops, so that they can be tasked with new objectives. 
<...>
7. In order to keep the details of the operation secret they shall be communicated only to a very narrow circle of absolutely necessary individuals, expanding this circle very slowly and as late as possible. This time – in any event – we should be careful enough to not let the enemy know anything of our plans.

<...>

11. To ensure the success of the advance, one of the key goals is to prevent the enemy from delaying the start of the operation by the possible advances against the Army Groups South and Center or force us to prematurely pull back the troops intended for the Citadel advance. 

This is why both army groups, in parallel with getting prepared for the operation, shall also by the end of the month get themselves ready for the possible defense at all other parts of the front.  At that it is especially important to quickly reinforce our positions and pay special attention to deploying AT gun positions, keep the local reserves at hand and organize recon activities to get the information on the most important concentrations of enemy forces.

[G009.FullName]
Battle of Kovel
[G009.Description]
On March 4, 1944 the Soviet Command ordered the 2nd Belorussian Front  to prepare and stage an advance operation with the key objective to move out their main forces to the Western Boug along the Brest - Gorodlo line, while moving the right flank forward to the Pripyat’ along the Turov - Davyd-Gorodok – Stolits line. The main strike was aimed at the town of Kovel — the most important rail- and motorway  intersection. Taking over the town would ensure the freedom of maneuver in the Brest, Kholmsk, and Vladimir-Volyn areas. After reaching the Western Boug, the Soviet troops were to make a deep flanking maneuver in the southern direction around the German Army Group Center, thus ensuring favorable positions to start the liberation of Poland and Belorussia. The advance was to begin on March 12-15.

The Germans had concentrated up to eight Divisions, including three Tank Divisions, one Brigade of ski-troops and five Assault Gun Brigades in the area. Starting from March 28, the command over the troops in the Kovel areas was transferred to the staff of the 2nd Army Group Center. The main efforts of the German army were aimed at unblocking the Kovel garrison. Having reinforced their Lyubomol-Kovel group with the troops and detachments of the 4th tank division, the Germans inflicted a concentrated strike onto the positions of the 143rd Rifle Division and, suffering substantial losses, broke the front open and by April 4 reached the Kovel area where they met with their encircled comrades.

[G010.FullName]
The Hunter - Villers Bocage
[G010.Description]
Early in the morning of June 13, 1944, the advance regiments of the 7th British Armored Division (the famous Desert Rats) drove down a curved highway, lined with chestnut trees on both sides, and entered Villers-Bocage, where they were met by the happy inhabitants. Around 9 a.m. some of the crews dismounted their machines, and the commander of the 4th battalion of the 22nd armored brigade, Lt.-Col. Lord Cranley (who arrived with a tank company of Cromwells, recon vehicles and a Motorized Infantry Company) decided to recon the road to Caen, which ran along the side of the forest, and his own final destination point - Height 213. On that same day Montgomery transmitted a telegram to his Chief-of-Staff De Guingand at the main command point in England indicating that he thought the German units around Caen were soon to succumb to his advances.

However, The fortunes of war were about to take a turn for the worse for the British around the small town of Villers-Bocage. 

That fortune would this day go to SS-Obersturmfuhrer Michael Wittmann – the commander of the 2nd company of the Schwere SS-Panzer-Abteilung 101. On June 7, the company left Beauvais and, having run under a severe air raid near Versailles on June 8, started moving only at nights, so that by June 12 they reached their deployment point. Wittmann and his men intended to spend the next day (June 13) repairing and fixing their tanks upon Height 213, not far from Villers-Bocage. After the previous air raids, Wittmann’s 2nd company was comprised of only four Tigers and one Panzer IV. 

Wittmann’s orders were to stop the advance of the British units, keep them out and away from the town and prevent them from flanking the German positions. They also had to defend the road to Caen. At 9am, Wittmann stood upon the turret of his Tiger tank and watched the British tank convoy in Villers-Bocage go about its business. 

"They behave like they’ve won the war already,” grumbled his gunner SS Rottenfuhrer Bobby Woll. “Well, we’ll have to point this mistake out to them,” Wittmann responded.

[G011.FullName]
The Mongoose
[G011.Description]
After the hard fought battles in Normandy, the Allies destroyed the garrison at Cherbourg and cast their gaze on the city of Saint Lo. This historic French town was the gateway to the German frontier and would be one of many goals for a new Allied offensive codenamed Operation Cobra. This operation would turnout to be a decisive turning point in France and for the war itself.

 
[G012.FullName]
Escaping the Pocket
[G012.Description]
The Allies are continuing to add to their hard-fought gains throughout France. While the initial Allied landings did surprise the German Command, the Germans have been able to create many strongpoint and defensive positions along the axis of advance and the Allied speed and momentum have begun to slow.

In the skies above, the Allied Air forces constantly pound the German units and deliver fiery destruction wherever they can. The German 7th Army has found itself nearly surrounded on all sides near the town of Falaise and have been fighting hard to escape the closing pocket.

[G014.FullName]
Wacht am Rhein
[G014.Description]
The Ardennes  - (southeastern Belgium) – December 1944-January 1945. The objective of a German operation codenamed Wacht am Rhein (Watch on the Rhine) was to advance a total of 25 Divisions (including seven Tank Divisions) across the Ardennes and onto Antwerp, defeat the Allies in Belgium and the Netherlands, thus changing the entire combat situation in the Western Front in favor of the Reich, and freeing up the Wehrmacht forces to fight against the USSR. 

On December 16, 1944, the troops and forces of the Waffen SS 6th Army, 5th Army and 7th Army – which formed the B group of armies under the command of Field-Marshal Model – began the advance. The four American Divisions under the command of General Bradley – which had to hold a 115-km line – were caught unawares and, suffering substantial losses, had to retreat. 

Having broken through the defenses of the Allies, by the end of December 17 the Germans advanced 20km forward; on December 19 they took Houffalize, approached Bastogne and already by the next day had almost completely encircled this fortified point. However the American troops in Bastogne managed to successfully repulse all of the attacks and hold the town until the arrival of the reinforcements, while the 7th Army, which was to cover the left flank of the advancing group, did not manage to break open American positions.

[G015.FullName]
The Last Frontier
[G015.Description]
The German command took all the necessary measures to prepare a deep fortified defense of Berlin. The geographic conditions were also in their favor. A multitude of lakes – interconnected with rivers and channels – made any advance with large tanks and heavy armor materiel very difficult. The shortened frontlines enabled the Germans to make an effective use of the natural obstacles; subsequently the defense did not require substantial numbers. The Germans had their positions fortified on the left side of the Oder and Neisse from Schwedt to Guben, where the depth of the engineered defense fortifications reached 30-40 km.  However the strongest defense lines were established at the approaches to Berlin, where the forces of the 1st Belorussian Front were to start their advance.  The defense fortifications here ran from the Oder and up to the very city of Berlin. 

The Germans have been accumulating their forces near Berlin throughout February, March and the beginning of April. Massive tank and motorized reserves, pulled back from the Western Front, were deployed in the back of the defenses. The makeup of Infantry Divisions was increased to nearly 8,000 men, while the Tank Divisions grew between 8,000 to 10,000 soldiers and officers. The troops and forces of the 1st Belorussian Front were facing nearly 50% of the entire German Berlin group. The German command paid special attention to the Oder bridgehead of the Red Army, where the forces of the 3rd and 5th Strike Armies and the 8th Guard Army were deployed. By the start of the Russian advance on April 16, 1945, the Germans had concentrated 48 Infantry, nine Motorized and six Tank Divisions, 37 separate Infantry Regiments, 98 separate Battalions and many more separate artillery detachments in the Berlin area. In addition to that, the Berlin garrison itself had over 200,000 men plus a reserve of eight more Divisions.